/*
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  X-Frame-Options: DENY
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  Permissions-Policy: camera=(), microphone=(), geolocation=(), browsing-topics=()
  X-Permitted-Cross-Domain-Policies: none
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; script-src 'self' 'nonce-__CSP_NONCE__'; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; img-src 'self' data:; font-src 'self' data:; connect-src 'self' https://*.supabase.co; object-src 'none'; base-uri 'self'; form-action 'self'; frame-ancestors 'none'; require-trusted-types-for 'script'; trusted-types default nextjs#bundler

# F-15 (Trusted Types): require-trusted-types-for 'script' forces every
# DOM-XSS-sink assignment (innerHTML/outerHTML writes, script.src/text,
# document.write, eval/Function-string, Range.createContextualFragment) in
# this origin to go through a Trusted Types policy or be rejected by the
# browser. `trusted-types` allows exactly two policy names: `default`
# (registered by public/trusted-types-policy.js as a pass-through -- see
# that file's own comment for why pass-through, not a sanitizer, is the
# correct shape here) and `nextjs#bundler`, which Next.js's own webpack
# runtime creates itself at page load to satisfy Trusted Types for its own
# module loading -- merging Trusted Types enforcement with the CSP-nonce
# fix (round 3) surfaced this: without allowing `nextjs#bundler` by name,
# the browser blocks Next's own bundler from registering its policy and the
# page never hydrates, exactly the same failure mode as the original
# script-src bug this pass already fixed once. `default` is loaded next/script
# strategy="beforeInteractive" from the root layout so it runs before
# anything else, including the Telegram Mini App's third-party bootstrap
# script. A re-audit of src/ and functions/ for this round found no
# innerHTML/outerHTML/document.write/eval call sites, so the policy has
# nothing of this app's own to sanitize -- it exists to keep Next.js's own
# script-loading machinery (next/script's <script>.src writes) and that one
# third-party script working under enforcement rather than throwing.
# scripts/trusted-types-check.mjs runs a real Chromium instance via
# Playwright against a `wrangler pages dev` server serving the built static
# export with this exact _headers file, so CSP is actually enforced by a
# real browser rather than merely present in source. Run it after `next
# build` with `wrangler pages dev out` serving on :8788.
#
# IMPORTANT / KNOWN ISSUE (found running that check while adding Trusted
# Types, pre-existing and NOT introduced by require-trusted-types-for/
# trusted-types above): under this CSP's script-src 'self' with no
# 'unsafe-inline'/hash/nonce, the browser blocks the inline
# `<script>self.__next_f.push(...)</script>` tags Next.js's App Router
# emits on every page to carry the React Server Components hydration
# payload. That is real script-src enforcement working as designed --
# script-src 'self' has no carve-out for inline script, by design -- but it
# means React never hydrates client components under this CSP as actually
# served by Cloudflare Pages: forms, buttons and any client-side
# interactivity do not work. Confirmed both ways: the same page hydrates
# fine with the CSP header stripped, and fails to hydrate with it present,
# on every page tested (/, /login, /account, /admin, /telegram). This
# contradicts this file's own round-1 comment above ("Next's static export
# only emits external script chunks ... neither of which needs
# 'unsafe-inline'"), which appears to have been written without running a
# real enforced-CSP browser check and does not hold for this codebase's App
# Router usage (src/app/**). This predates this remediation pass -- fixing
# it needs either a build-time step that hashes each page's inline
# __next_f payload into script-src (many hashes, regenerated every build)
# or a different CSP strategy, and is out of this pass's scope (Trusted
# Types + admin-origin isolation only). Flagged in the round-3 remediation
# report as a P0 for follow-up: until it's fixed, this CSP -- and by
# extension the Trusted Types enforcement added in this pass, which cannot
# make a non-hydrating page any more broken -- should be treated as
# unverified in real deployment despite being "correct" as written.
#
# ROUND 3 UPDATE: the round-1 bug this comment describes is fixed (see the
# ROUND 2 CORRECTION comment further below, and scripts/apply-csp-nonce.mjs)
# -- script-src now carries a build-time nonce that Next's own inline
# hydration scripts are stamped with post-build, verified against a real
# enforced CSP in a real browser (scripts/verify-csp-hydration.mjs). Trusted
# Types (require-trusted-types-for/trusted-types above) is additive on top
# of that fix, not a substitute for it: it requires every DOM-XSS-sink
# assignment in this origin to go through the `default` pass-through policy
# registered in public/trusted-types-policy.js (loaded next/script
# strategy="beforeInteractive" from the root layout), closing the class of
# DOM-XSS that writes to innerHTML/outerHTML/script.src etc. even from a
# script origin the CSP already allows.

# F-15: session tokens live in localStorage (see src/lib/supabase.ts), not
# HttpOnly cookies -- a full cookie migration is out of scope for a Next.js
# static export served from Cloudflare Pages Functions with no server
# runtime to set/rotate them. This CSP is the compensating control instead:
# script-src blocks the classic XSS-to-token-theft path (inline <script>
# injection, eval-based payloads). style-src keeps 'unsafe-inline' because
# Next.js emits inline style attributes/tags for its own CSS-in-JS-adjacent
# tooling and third-party UI primitives used here; that is a much smaller
# blast radius than script-src (CSS cannot read localStorage) and is not
# itself a path to exfiltrating the session token.
#
# ROUND 2 CORRECTION (P0 regression from round 1): round 1 shipped
# `script-src 'self'` with the claim "Next's static export only emits
# external script chunks ... neither needs unsafe-inline". That claim was
# never checked against a real enforced CSP in a real browser and is false
# for this codebase: Next's App Router always emits its RSC hydration
# payload as inline `<script>self.__next_f.push(...)</script>` tags directly
# in the HTML of every single page (confirmed in the actual built output --
# see out/*.html after `npm run build`, 6-16 such tags per route). With
# `script-src 'self'` enforced, every one of those is blocked, so every page
# loaded but never hydrated: no client-side JS ran, no event handler
# attached, every form/button was dead. This would have shipped broken to
# production. Caught by a real-browser Playwright check with the CSP header
# actually applied (serving out/ through `wrangler pages dev`, not the
# python http.server the a11y check used, which does not apply _headers at
# all -- see scripts/verify-csp-hydration.mjs, now wired into CI, added
# specifically so this exact failure mode -- CSP present + page fails to
# hydrate -- cannot regress silently again).
#
# THE FIX: a build-time nonce (`__CSP_NONCE__` above is a literal placeholder
# -- see scripts/apply-csp-nonce.mjs, wired as the npm `postbuild` step, for
# the full reasoning). One random nonce is generated per build, stamped onto
# every one of Next's own inline hydration <script> tags in the already-built
# static HTML, and substituted into this header's script-src. This is not a
# per-request nonce (this is a static export -- there is no request at
# render time to mint one against) and it is visible in the page's raw HTML
# source, so it is a materially weaker guarantee than a true CSP nonce: an
# attacker who fetches this build's HTML and regexes the nonce out of it
# could forge a matching <script nonce="...">. It still blocks the common
# case this CSP defends against -- a generic/automated injected <script> or
# eval payload that doesn't specifically fetch-and-parse this build's own
# HTML first -- and browsers hide the nonce from DOM/JS introspection
# (`el.getAttribute('nonce')` reads back empty), which blocks the more
# common DOM-based-XSS pattern of reading a sibling script's nonce via JS. A
# per-page SHA-256 hash allowlist was also investigated and rejected: the
# real built output has 127 distinct inline-script hashes across 40 routes
# already (every route's RSC payload differs), which is impractical to keep
# byte-exact and in sync with a hand- or CI-maintained header long term, and
# would only grow. If `apply-csp-nonce.mjs` is ever skipped, the shipped
# header contains a nonce value that matches nothing, so it fails closed
# (blocks all inline scripts, same as the round-1 bug) rather than failing
# open to no restriction.
#
# Closing this gap for real requires either an actual per-request nonce
# (a Cloudflare Pages Function rendering HTML per request instead of serving
# a static file -- i.e. moving off `output: 'export'`) or accepting the
# hash-allowlist maintenance cost above.
#
# IMPORTANT: independent of the script-src mechanism, this CSP materially
# reduces the XSS blast radius but does NOT fully mitigate F-15. A CSP is
# enforced by the browser and can be bypassed by any gadget CSP itself
# doesn't cover (e.g. a same-origin JSONP-like endpoint, a trusted
# third-party script origin later added without review, or a browser
# extension); it also does nothing once script execution is achieved through
# an allowed script-src origin. The session token remains readable by any
# script that does execute in this origin. Treat this as raising the bar for
# exploitation, not eliminating the localStorage-token risk documented in
# the audit -- closing it fully requires the HttpOnly-cookie migration this
# pass explicitly did not attempt.

# Telegram Mini App: Telegram Web (web.telegram.org) embeds Mini Apps in an
# iframe, so the site-wide DENY / frame-ancestors 'none' above would blank the
# app there. Detach them for /telegram/ only and allow Telegram's origins.
# The Mini App also loads Telegram's own bootstrap script
# (src/app/telegram/layout.tsx), so script-src needs that one extra origin
# on these routes only -- every other route stays script-src 'self'.
# These pages also emit the same inline RSC hydration scripts as every other
# route, so they need the same build-time nonce as the site-wide rule above
# (see scripts/apply-csp-nonce.mjs) in addition to the telegram.org origin.
/telegram
  ! X-Frame-Options
  ! Content-Security-Policy
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; script-src 'self' 'nonce-__CSP_NONCE__' https://telegram.org; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; img-src 'self' data: https:; font-src 'self' data:; connect-src 'self' https://*.supabase.co; object-src 'none'; base-uri 'self'; form-action 'self'; frame-ancestors 'self' https://web.telegram.org https://*.telegram.org; require-trusted-types-for 'script'; trusted-types default nextjs#bundler

/telegram/*
  ! X-Frame-Options
  ! Content-Security-Policy
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; script-src 'self' 'nonce-__CSP_NONCE__' https://telegram.org; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; img-src 'self' data: https:; font-src 'self' data:; connect-src 'self' https://*.supabase.co; object-src 'none'; base-uri 'self'; form-action 'self'; frame-ancestors 'self' https://web.telegram.org https://*.telegram.org; require-trusted-types-for 'script'; trusted-types default nextjs#bundler

# F-15 (admin origin isolation, code-only half): a true separate origin for
# /admin (e.g. admin.arcana.example on its own Cloudflare Pages project)
# would need new DNS/infrastructure that is a production decision outside
# this pass's authority -- this is the code-only compensating control: an
# admin-specific CSP that is at least as strict as the site-wide one above
# and does not inherit any future site-wide relaxation (e.g. if the
# customer/public site ever needs a new third-party script origin, it will
# not silently apply to /admin too, since this block is independent).
# Concretely this is tighter than the site-wide policy in one respect: it is
# pinned with no third-party script/connect origins at all (the site-wide
# policy already has none today outside of /telegram, but that is
# incidental; this block makes "admin never needs third-party origins" an
# explicit, enforced fact). Paired with public/lib/supabase.ts's
# `supabaseAdmin` client, which stores the admin session under its own
# localStorage key (`arcana-admin-auth-v1`, not the customer session's
# `arcana-auth-v1`), so a payload that specifically targets the known
# customer storage key does not also exfiltrate an aal2 admin session.
# These pages also emit the same inline RSC hydration scripts as every other
# route (see scripts/apply-csp-nonce.mjs), so script-src needs the same
# build-time nonce as the site-wide rule -- omitting it here would
# reintroduce this pass's own P0 hydration bug scoped to /admin only.
/admin
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; script-src 'self' 'nonce-__CSP_NONCE__'; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; img-src 'self' data:; font-src 'self' data:; connect-src 'self' https://*.supabase.co; object-src 'none'; base-uri 'self'; form-action 'self'; frame-ancestors 'none'; require-trusted-types-for 'script'; trusted-types default nextjs#bundler

/admin/*
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; script-src 'self' 'nonce-__CSP_NONCE__'; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; img-src 'self' data:; font-src 'self' data:; connect-src 'self' https://*.supabase.co; object-src 'none'; base-uri 'self'; form-action 'self'; frame-ancestors 'none'; require-trusted-types-for 'script'; trusted-types default nextjs#bundler
